**Solution Explanation**

For a sequence `A = (A1 , … , AN)` we have to answer `Q` queries

```
ik  xk          ( 1 ≤ ik ≤ N )
```

* first `Aik` becomes `xk`
* then we have to output  

```
mex(A) = the smallest non‑negative integer that does not appear in A
```

`N , Q ≤ 2·10^5`, the values of `Ai , xk` are up to `10^9`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.  Observations  

* The mex of a sequence of length `N` is never larger than `N`.  
  If all numbers `0 … N‑1` are present, the answer is `N`; otherwise it is
  some smaller number.
* Therefore we only have to know **how many times each value `v` with  
  `0 ≤ v ≤ N` occurs**.  
  All larger values are irrelevant for the mex.

If we know for every `v ∈ [0 , N]` whether its count is zero or not,
the mex is simply the smallest `v` whose count is zero.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.  Data structures  

* `cnt[0 … N]` – integer array, `cnt[v]` = how many times `v` occurs in the
  current sequence (only for `v ≤ N`).
* A segment tree (or a binary indexed tree with binary lifting) that
  can return the **minimum index whose count is zero**.

  For each leaf `v`

```
value(v) = v          if cnt[v] == 0
           INF        otherwise          (INF = N+5, larger than any answer)
```

  An internal node stores the minimum of its two children.
  The value stored in the root is exactly the current mex.

Both structures support point updates in `O(log N)` time.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Algorithm  

```
read N , Q
read array A[0 … N-1]

cnt[0 … N] = 0
for each element a in A
        if a ≤ N : cnt[a]++

build segment tree on indices 0 … N
        leaf v gets (cnt[v]==0 ? v : INF)

for each query (i , x)               // i is 1‑based
        old = A[i-1]

        // remove the old value
        if old ≤ N
                cnt[old]--
                if cnt[old]==0   update leaf old to old
                else             update leaf old to INF

        // insert the new value
        A[i-1] = x
        if x ≤ N
                cnt[x]++
                if cnt[x]==1    update leaf x to INF   // it was zero before
                // otherwise leaf stays INF

        answer = tree.root_value          // the mex
        output answer
```

All operations are `O(log N)`, therefore the whole program works in  
`O((N+Q) log N)` time and `O(N)` memory.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs the correct mex after every query.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For every `v ∈ [0 , N]` the segment‑tree leaf `v` stores `v`
iff `cnt[v] = 0`; otherwise it stores `INF`.

**Proof.**  
The leaf is set exactly according to this rule during the initial build
and after each update of `cnt[v]` we immediately rewrite the leaf
following the same rule. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
At any moment the value stored in the root of the segment tree equals  

```
min { v ∈ [0 , N] | cnt[v] = 0 } .
```

**Proof.**  
By Lemma&nbsp;1 each leaf contains either its index `v` (if `cnt[v]=0`) or
`INF` (otherwise).  
An internal node stores the minimum of its two children, therefore every
node stores the minimum index with zero count inside its interval.
The root covers the whole interval `[0 , N]`, hence its value is the
minimum index with zero count in the whole array. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
After processing a query, `cnt[v]` equals the number of occurrences of
`v` (for `v ≤ N`) in the current sequence `A`.

**Proof.**  
Only the element at position `i` changes.
If the old value `old` is ≤ N we decrement `cnt[old]`; this exactly removes
one occurrence of `old`.  
If the new value `x` is ≤ N we increment `cnt[x]`; this adds one occurrence
of `x`.  
All other `cnt` entries stay unchanged, therefore the invariant holds. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
After processing a query, the root value of the segment tree equals
`mex(A)` of the current sequence.

**Proof.**  
By Lemma&nbsp;3, after the update `cnt[v]` is the true multiplicity of `v`
in `A` for every `v ≤ N`.  
Consequently the set `{ v ∈ [0 , N] | cnt[v] = 0 }` is exactly the set of
non‑negative integers not present in `A` (restricted to `0 … N`).  
The mex of `A` is the smallest non‑negative integer missing from `A`;
if all `0 … N‑1` are present, the mex is `N`.  
Thus the mex equals the minimum element of the above set, which by
Lemma&nbsp;2 is the root value. ∎



##### Theorem  
For each query the algorithm outputs the correct mex of the sequence after
the update.

**Proof.**  
When a query finishes, the algorithm reads the root value of the segment
tree and prints it.  
By Lemma&nbsp;4 this value equals `mex(A)` of the current sequence, therefore
the printed number is correct. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.  Complexity Analysis  

*Building*  

* counting initial frequencies: `O(N)`
* building the segment tree: `O(N)`

*Each query*  

* at most two point updates of the segment tree: `2·O(log N)`
* reading the root: `O(1)`

Total time: `O((N + Q) log N)`  
Memory: `cnt` (`N+1` integers) + segment tree (`4·(N+1)` integers) = `O(N)`.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 6.  Reference Implementation  (C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct SegTree {
    const int INF;
    int n;                     // size of the base interval (power of two)
    vector<int> seg;           // 2*n elements
    SegTree(int sz, int inf) : INF(inf) {
        n = 1;
        while (n < sz) n <<= 1;
        seg.assign(2 * n, INF);
    }
    // set leaf at position p (0‑based) to value v, then recompute upwards
    void set_leaf(int p, int v) {
        p += n;
        seg[p] = v;
        for (p >>= 1; p; p >>= 1)
            seg[p] = min(seg[p << 1], seg[p << 1 | 1]);
    }
    // whole interval minimum (the mex)
    int all_min() const { return seg[1]; }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int N, Q;
    if (!(cin >> N >> Q)) return 0;
    vector<long long> A(N);
    for (auto &x : A) cin >> x;

    const int INF = N + 5;                 // larger than any possible answer
    vector<int> cnt(N + 1, 0);             // counts for values 0 … N

    for (auto x : A)
        if (0 <= x && x <= N) cnt[(int)x]++;

    SegTree st(N + 1, INF);
    for (int v = 0; v <= N; ++v)
        st.set_leaf(v, cnt[v] == 0 ? v : INF);

    for (int qq = 0; qq < Q; ++qq) {
        int idx; long long x;
        cin >> idx >> x;
        --idx;                              // to 0‑based
        long long old = A[idx];

        // remove old value
        if (0 <= old && old <= N) {
            int v = (int)old;
            --cnt[v];
            if (cnt[v] == 0) st.set_leaf(v, v);
            else            st.set_leaf(v, INF);
        }

        // insert new value
        A[idx] = x;
        if (0 <= x && x <= N) {
            int v = (int)x;
            ++cnt[v];
            if (cnt[v] == 1) st.set_leaf(v, INF);   // it was zero before
            // otherwise leaf already INF, nothing to do
        }

        cout << st.all_min() << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above and
conforms to the GNU++17 compiler.

## Solution

The important observation is:

> For an array of length `N`, the mex is always between `0` and `N`.

This is because if all numbers `0, 1, ..., N` were present, we would need at least `N + 1` elements.

So we only need to care about values in the range `[0, N]`.  
Values greater than `N` do not affect the mex.

---

## Approach

Maintain:

- `A`: the current array.
- `cnt[v]`: how many times value `v` appears in `A`, for `0 <= v <= N`.
- `missing`: a set containing all values `v` in `[0, N]` such that `cnt[v] == 0`.

Then the mex is simply:

```cpp
*missing.begin()
```

For each update `(i, x)`:

1. Let `old = A[i]`.
2. If `old <= N`, decrease `cnt[old]`.
   - If it becomes `0`, insert `old` into `missing`.
3. If `x <= N`, increase `cnt[x]`.
   - Before increasing, if `cnt[x] == 0`, erase `x` from `missing`.
4. Update `A[i] = x`.
5. Output `*missing.begin()`.

---

## Correctness Proof

We maintain the invariant:

> For every `v` with `0 <= v <= N`, `cnt[v]` is exactly the number of occurrences of `v` in the current array, and `missing` contains exactly those `v` with `cnt[v] == 0`.

Initially, we build `cnt` from the initial array and insert every value with count `0` into `missing`, so the invariant holds.

For an update, only one array element changes, from `old` to `x`.

- If `old <= N`, we decrement its count. If the count becomes `0`, `old` is now missing, so inserting it into `missing` preserves the invariant.
- If `x <= N`, then after the update `x` appears one more time. If its count was `0`, it was missing, so we remove it from `missing`. Then we increment its count.

All other counts remain unchanged, so the invariant still holds after the update.

Since the mex of an array of length `N` is always in `[0, N]`, and `missing` contains exactly the missing values in this range, the smallest element of `missing` is exactly the mex.

Therefore, the algorithm outputs the correct mex after every query.

---

## Complexity

Let `N` be the array length and `Q` the number of queries.

- Initialization: `O(N log N)`
- Each query: `O(log N)`
- Memory usage: `O(N)`

This fits the constraints.

---

## C++ Code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N, Q;
    cin >> N >> Q;

    vector<int> A(N + 1);
    vector<int> cnt(N + 1, 0);

    for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
        cin >> A[i];
        if (A[i] <= N) {
            cnt[A[i]]++;
        }
    }

    set<int> missing;
    for (int v = 0; v <= N; v++) {
        if (cnt[v] == 0) {
            missing.insert(v);
        }
    }

    while (Q--) {
        int i, x;
        cin >> i >> x;

        int old = A[i];

        if (old != x) {
            if (old <= N) {
                cnt[old]--;
                if (cnt[old] == 0) {
                    missing.insert(old);
                }
            }

            if (x <= N) {
                if (cnt[x] == 0) {
                    missing.erase(x);
                }
                cnt[x]++;
            }

            A[i] = x;
        }

        cout << *missing.begin() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

## Solution

Root the tree at vertex \(1\).

For a set of specified vertices \(S\), the minimum closed walk from \(1\) that visits all vertices in \(S\) is exactly twice the total length of the minimal subtree connecting \(1\) and \(S\).  
Every edge in that subtree must be traversed at least twice, and a DFS-like walk achieves that.

So Aoki wants to choose \(K\) vertices maximizing the total length of edges covered by the union of their paths from the root.

Because all edge lengths are positive, for \(K\) up to the number of rooted leaves, an optimal choice can be made only from rooted leaves. If \(K\) is larger than the number of rooted leaves, choosing all leaves already covers the whole tree, so the answer becomes twice the total tree length.

---

## Key idea: marginal gains and heavy paths

For a rooted subtree \(v\), consider the marginal gains obtained by choosing leaves in that subtree.

If \(v\) is a leaf, its marginal sequence is:

\[
[0]
\]

For a child \(c\) of \(v\), connected by an edge of length \(w\), if we choose at least one leaf inside \(c\)'s subtree, we must also take that edge \(w\). Therefore, if the marginal sequence of \(c\) is

\[
[a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots]
\]

then its contribution sequence from \(v\)'s perspective is:

\[
[w + a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots]
\]

When combining children, since all sequences are non-increasing, the optimal marginal sequence for \(v\) is just the sorted merge of the children's contribution sequences.

Let

\[
down[v] = \text{maximum distance from } v \text{ to a leaf in its rooted subtree}
\]

and choose a **heavy child** of \(v\) as a child maximizing

\[
\text{edge length} + down[\text{child}]
\]

Then the optimal marginal gains for the whole tree are exactly the lengths of the heavy-path decomposition:

- A heavy path starts at the root or at a child that is **not** the heavy child of its parent.
- It follows heavy children until a leaf.
- Its length includes the incoming edge from the parent if the path starts at a non-root vertex.

More concretely, the marginal gains are:

\[
down[1]
\]

and for every vertex \(v \neq 1\) that is **not** the heavy child of its parent:

\[
\text{edge length}(parent[v], v) + down[v]
\]

Sort these values in decreasing order. Their prefix sums are the maximum covered lengths for \(K = 1, 2, \ldots\). The answer is twice those sums.

---

## Complexity

- Rooting the tree and computing `down` and `heavy`: \(O(N)\)
- Sorting at most \(N\) heavy-path lengths: \(O(N \log N)\)
- Memory: \(O(N)\)

---

## C++ Code

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

using ll = long long;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    cin >> N;

    vector<vector<pair<int, ll>>> adj(N + 1);
    ll total = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < N - 1; i++) {
        int u, v;
        ll l;
        cin >> u >> v >> l;
        adj[u].push_back({v, l});
        adj[v].push_back({u, l});
        total += l;
    }

    // Root the tree at 1.
    vector<int> parent(N + 1, 0), order;
    vector<ll> parent_w(N + 1, 0);
    order.reserve(N);

    parent[1] = -1;
    vector<int> st;
    st.reserve(N);
    st.push_back(1);

    while (!st.empty()) {
        int v = st.back();
        st.pop_back();
        order.push_back(v);

        for (auto [to, w] : adj[v]) {
            if (to == parent[v]) continue;
            if (parent[to] != 0) continue;

            parent[to] = v;
            parent_w[to] = w;
            st.push_back(to);
        }
    }

    // down[v] = longest distance from v to a leaf in its rooted subtree.
    // heavy[v] = child achieving down[v].
    vector<ll> down(N + 1, 0);
    vector<int> heavy(N + 1, -1);

    for (int i = (int)order.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        int v = order[i];
        ll best = -1;

        for (auto [to, w] : adj[v]) {
            if (parent[to] == v) {
                ll val = down[to] + w;
                if (val > best) {
                    best = val;
                    down[v] = val;
                    heavy[v] = to;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // Collect heavy-path lengths.
    vector<ll> gains;
    gains.reserve(N);

    // Heavy path starting at the root.
    gains.push_back(down[1]);

    // Heavy paths starting at light children.
    for (int v = 2; v <= N; v++) {
        if (heavy[parent[v]] != v) {
            gains.push_back(parent_w[v] + down[v]);
        }
    }

    sort(gains.begin(), gains.end(), greater<ll>());

    ll cur = 0;
    int idx = 0;

    for (int k = 1; k <= N; k++) {
        if (idx < (int)gains.size()) {
            cur += gains[idx++];
            cout << cur * 2 << '\n';
        } else {
            // All rooted leaves are already chosen: whole tree is covered.
            cout << total * 2 << '\n';
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
