## Idea

Make a directed graph where an edge `p -> i` means:  
“book `p` must be read before book `i`.”

To read book `1`, you must read all books that can reach book `1` in this graph, i.e. all ancestors of book `1`.  
This set is unique. After finding it, output any topological order of those books, excluding book `1`.

---

## Algorithm

1. Read input.
   - `prereq[i]`: prerequisites of book `i`
   - `depend[p]`: books that require book `p`

2. Find all required books:
   - Start DFS/BFS from book `1`, following `prereq` backwards.
   - Mark every visited book as `need`.

3. Topologically sort only the required books:
   - Compute indegrees inside the required subgraph.
   - Run Kahn’s algorithm.
   - Do not print book `1`.

---

## Correctness

A book is necessary for reading book `1` iff it is an ancestor of book `1` in the prerequisite graph.

- If a book is an ancestor of book `1`, it appears in some prerequisite chain leading to book `1`, so it must be read.
- If a book is not an ancestor of book `1`, it is not needed directly or indirectly, so reading it is unnecessary.

Thus the minimum required set is exactly the ancestors of book `1`, excluding book `1`.

Since it is possible to read all books, the graph is acyclic. Therefore a topological order exists. Kahn’s algorithm on the required subgraph outputs an order where every book appears after all its required prerequisites. Hence the printed order is valid.

---

## Complexity

Let

- `N` be the number of books
- `M = sum C_i` be the total number of prerequisite relations

Time complexity: `O(N + M)`  
Memory complexity: `O(N + M)`

---

## C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    cin >> N;

    vector<vector<int>> prereq(N + 1), depend(N + 1);

    for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
        int C;
        cin >> C;

        for (int j = 0; j < C; j++) {
            int p;
            cin >> p;

            prereq[i].push_back(p);
            depend[p].push_back(i);
        }
    }

    // Find all books required for book 1.
    vector<char> need(N + 1, false);
    vector<int> st;

    st.push_back(1);
    need[1] = true;

    while (!st.empty()) {
        int u = st.back();
        st.pop_back();

        for (int p : prereq[u]) {
            if (!need[p]) {
                need[p] = true;
                st.push_back(p);
            }
        }
    }

    // Compute indegrees inside the required subgraph.
    vector<int> indeg(N + 1, 0);

    for (int u = 1; u <= N; u++) {
        if (!need[u]) continue;

        for (int p : prereq[u]) {
            if (need[p]) {
                indeg[u]++;
            }
        }
    }

    // Kahn's algorithm on required books.
    queue<int> q;

    for (int u = 1; u <= N; u++) {
        if (need[u] && indeg[u] == 0) {
            q.push(u);
        }
    }

    vector<int> ans;

    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front();
        q.pop();

        if (u != 1) {
            ans.push_back(u);
        }

        for (int v : depend[u]) {
            if (!need[v]) continue;

            indeg[v]--;

            if (indeg[v] == 0) {
                q.push(v);
            }
        }
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < (int)ans.size(); i++) {
        if (i) cout << ' ';
        cout << ans[i];
    }
    cout << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

## Approach

For a fixed value \(x\), suppose the maximum \(A_i\) among the chosen \(K\) elements is at most \(x\).  
Then we can only use indices with \(A_i \le x\), and to minimize

\[
\left(\max_{i\in S} A_i\right)\times \left(\sum_{i\in S} B_i\right)
\]

for this fixed \(x\), we should choose the \(K\) smallest \(B_i\) among those indices.

So, after sorting elements by \(A_i\) ascending, we process prefixes and maintain the sum of the \(K\) smallest \(B_i\) seen so far.

For each distinct value \(a\) of \(A_i\), after inserting all elements with \(A_i=a\):

- if at least \(K\) elements have been seen,
- candidate answer is  
  \[
  a \times (\text{sum of } K \text{ smallest } B_i \text{ among seen elements})
  \]

Take the minimum over all such candidates.

To maintain the \(K\) smallest \(B_i\), use a max-heap:

- store the currently chosen \(K\) smallest \(B_i\)
- the heap top is the largest among them
- when a new \(B_i\) is smaller than the top, replace the top with the new value

---

## Correctness

Let \(G(x)\) be the sum of the \(K\) smallest \(B_i\) among indices with \(A_i \le x\), if there are at least \(K\) such indices.

For any valid subset \(S\), let

\[
m = \max_{i\in S} A_i
\]

Then all elements of \(S\) satisfy \(A_i \le m\), so

\[
\sum_{i\in S} B_i \ge G(m)
\]

Therefore,

\[
\left(\max_{i\in S} A_i\right)\left(\sum_{i\in S} B_i\right)
\ge m \cdot G(m)
\]

Thus the answer is at least

\[
\min_x x \cdot G(x)
\]

Conversely, for any \(x\) with at least \(K\) eligible elements, choose the \(K\) indices with smallest \(B_i\) among \(A_i \le x\).  
Their actual maximum \(A_i\) is some \(y \le x\), so their value is

\[
y \cdot G(x) \le x \cdot G(x)
\]

Hence the optimum is at most \(\min_x x \cdot G(x)\).

So the optimum equals

\[
\min_x x \cdot G(x)
\]

The algorithm computes exactly these values while scanning \(A_i\) in ascending order.

---

## Complexity

For each test case:

- Sorting: \(O(N \log N)\)
- Heap operations: \(O(N \log K)\)

Since the sum of \(N\) over all test cases is at most \(2 \times 10^5\), this is efficient.

Memory usage: \(O(N)\).

---

## C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int T;
    cin >> T;

    while (T--) {
        int N, K;
        cin >> N >> K;

        vector<int> A(N);
        for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
            cin >> A[i];
        }

        vector<pair<int, int>> items(N);
        for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
            int b;
            cin >> b;
            items[i] = {A[i], b};
        }

        sort(items.begin(), items.end());

        // Max-heap storing the current K smallest B values.
        priority_queue<int> pq;

        long long sum = 0;
        long long ans = LLONG_MAX;

        int i = 0;
        while (i < N) {
            int a = items[i].first;

            // Insert all elements having the same A value.
            while (i < N && items[i].first == a) {
                int b = items[i].second;

                if ((int)pq.size() < K) {
                    pq.push(b);
                    sum += b;
                } else if (b < pq.top()) {
                    sum -= pq.top();
                    pq.pop();
                    pq.push(b);
                    sum += b;
                }

                ++i;
            }

            if ((int)pq.size() == K) {
                ans = min(ans, 1LL * a * sum);
            }
        }

        cout << ans << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
```

