## Key observation

The conditions say exactly that `A_i` is the **nearest previous position whose value is smaller** than `P_i`.

Indeed:

- If `A_i > 0`, then `P_{A_i} < P_i`.
- Every position strictly between `A_i` and `i` has value larger than `P_i`.
- If `A_i = 0`, all previous positions have value larger than `P_i`.

So `A_i` is the previous smaller element of position `i`, with `0` meaning “none”.

This is precisely the information encoded by the **minimum Cartesian tree** of the permutation.

---

## Cartesian tree view

For a permutation `P`, its minimum Cartesian tree is the unique binary tree such that:

1. Its inorder traversal is `1, 2, ..., N`.
2. Values satisfy the heap property: a parent has a smaller value than its children.

For this tree, the previous smaller element of a node `i` is the nearest ancestor of `i` that lies to the left of `i`; if there is no such ancestor, it is `0`.

Thus the sequence `A` uniquely determines the shape of the Cartesian tree, and the required permutations are exactly the heap labelings of that tree.

---

## Reconstructing the tree from `A`

Build the Cartesian tree online using a stack.  
The stack stores the current right spine of the Cartesian tree, with sentinel `0` at the bottom.

For each `i = 1, ..., N`:

- Pop nodes until the stack top becomes `A_i`.
- Let `last` be the last popped node.
- If `last != 0`, it becomes the left child of `i`.
- If `A_i != 0`, `i` becomes the right child of `A_i`.
- If `A_i == 0`, `i` becomes the new root.
- Push `i`.

Because the input is guaranteed to be feasible, `A_i` is always on the stack.

This is exactly the standard Cartesian tree construction, except that we do not know the values; `A_i` tells us where the popping stops.

---

## Counting heap labelings

For a rooted tree, the number of ways to assign distinct values `1..N` such that every parent is smaller than its children is the tree hook-length formula:

\[
\text{answer} = \frac{N!}{\prod_v \text{subtree\_size}(v)}
\]

Modulo `998244353`, division is multiplication by modular inverse.

So:

\[
\text{answer} \equiv N! \cdot \left(\prod_v \text{subtree\_size}(v)\right)^{-1}
\pmod{998244353}
\]

Since `N <= 3e5 < 998244353`, all subtree sizes are invertible modulo `998244353`.

---

## Complexity

- Tree construction: `O(N)`
- Subtree size computation: `O(N)`
- Total: `O(N)`
- Memory: `O(N)`

---

## C++ implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

using ll = long long;
constexpr ll MOD = 998244353;

ll modpow(ll a, ll e) {
    ll r = 1;
    while (e > 0) {
        if (e & 1) r = r * a % MOD;
        a = a * a % MOD;
        e >>= 1;
    }
    return r;
}

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    cin >> N;

    vector<int> left_child(N + 1, 0), right_child(N + 1, 0);

    // Stack for Cartesian tree construction.
    // 0 is a sentinel representing the imaginary position before 1.
    vector<int> st;
    st.reserve(N + 1);
    st.push_back(0);

    int root = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
        int a;
        cin >> a;

        int last = 0;

        // The input is guaranteed feasible, so a is always in the stack.
        while (st.back() != a) {
            last = st.back();
            st.pop_back();
        }

        if (last != 0) {
            left_child[i] = last;
        }

        if (a != 0) {
            right_child[a] = i;
        } else {
            root = i;
        }

        st.push_back(i);
    }

    // Compute subtree sizes iteratively to avoid recursion depth issues.
    vector<int> order;
    order.reserve(N);

    vector<int> dfs_stack;
    dfs_stack.reserve(N);
    dfs_stack.push_back(root);

    while (!dfs_stack.empty()) {
        int v = dfs_stack.back();
        dfs_stack.pop_back();

        order.push_back(v);

        if (left_child[v] != 0) dfs_stack.push_back(left_child[v]);
        if (right_child[v] != 0) dfs_stack.push_back(right_child[v]);
    }

    vector<int> subtree_size(N + 1, 0);

    for (int i = (int)order.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        int v = order[i];
        subtree_size[v] = 1;
        if (left_child[v] != 0) subtree_size[v] += subtree_size[left_child[v]];
        if (right_child[v] != 0) subtree_size[v] += subtree_size[right_child[v]];
    }

    ll fact = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
        fact = fact * i % MOD;
    }

    ll denom = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
        denom = denom * subtree_size[i] % MOD;
    }

    ll ans = fact * modpow(denom, MOD - 2) % MOD;
    cout << ans << '\n';

    return 0;
}
```

